 "caption": "Death is killing everyone in Pieter Bruegel's The Triumph of Death (c. 1562). An army of skeletons sweeps the land and rings the bell for the end of the world. A king's gold is looted, a cardinal is led away, a starving dog nibbles at a dead child.\n\nExcept in the bottom right corner. A young man plays the lute, a woman sings from a sheet of music, and they see only each other. Behind them, a skeleton is already playing along.\n\nIn 1960 Sylvia Plath published \"Two Views of a Cadaver Room\" (The Nation, 30 January 1960). It moves from a dissecting room to this very corner: \"Two people only are blind to the carrion army\". And then their sentence: \"These Flemish lovers flourish; not for long.\"\n\nP.S. The painting has been in the Prado since 1827.\n\n🖤 Would you have noticed the skeleton behind them?",
 "caption_short": "Death is killing everyone in this Bruegel. Except two lovers. Sylvia Plath gave them their sentence. 🖤",
